<body>
<header><div class="wrap">
<div class="eyebrow">PathKeeper • Website Operations</div>
<h1>Page Content & Link Map</h1>
<p>A practical editing blueprint for deciding what each PathKeeper page should do, what information belongs on it, and which buttons should lead visitors to the next useful step.</p>
</div></header>
<main class="wrap">
<section class="stats">
<div class="stat"><div class="num" id="pageCount">32</div><div class="muted">Page / topic entries in this draft</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="num" id="coreCount">8</div><div class="muted">Suggested core destinations</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="num" id="linkCount">0</div><div class="muted">Suggested link relationships</div></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="num">4</div><div class="muted">Visitor journey stages</div></div>
</section>
<section class="panel">
<h2>Editing decisions first</h2>
<p class="muted">The published Editing Map and Home pages expose project notes and button labels, but the individual page content and private Google Sites Pages panel are not fully readable from the public view. The descriptions below are proposed content based on the existing titles—not claims that those sections already exist.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Preservation rule:</b> Do not delete or overwrite a page just because it appears duplicated. First compare its unique content and outbound links, save a copy or move the content to a safe draft, then decide whether to merge, rename, or retain it.</div>
<div class="flow">
<div class="step"><b>1 · Discover</b>Help visitors find the right topic or opportunity.</div>
<div class="step"><b>2 · Understand</b>Explain the facts, requirements, trade-offs, and resources.</div>
<div class="step"><b>3 · Decide</b>Help the visitor choose a realistic next step.</div>
<div class="step"><b>4 · Act & prove</b>Track actions and produce evidence of progress.</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="panel">
<h2>Page inventory with content to add</h2>
<p class="muted">Search or filter this table. Use the recommendations as the working content brief for each page. Confirm actual page names and URLs against the Google Sites Pages panel.</p>
<div class="controls no-print">
<input id="search" placeholder="Search page title, purpose, content, or button…">
<select id="groupFilter"><option value="">All groups</option></select>
<select id="statusFilter"><option value="">All editing decisions</option><option>Keep</option><option>Review</option><option>Merge</option><option>Rename</option><option>Move</option><option>Create</option></select>
</div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Page title</th><th>Role / purpose</th><th>Information to include</th><th>Recommended buttons / links</th><th>Parent / group</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody id="rows"></tbody></table></div>
<div class="actions no-print"><button onclick="downloadCSV()">Export page briefs (CSV)</button><button class="alt" onclick="window.print()">Print / save as PDF</button></div>
</section>
<section class="panel">
<h2>Recommended main navigation</h2>
<div class="cards" id="coreCards"></div>
</section>
<section class="panel">
<h2>Button and cross-link plan</h2>
<p class="muted">These are suggested destinations. In Google Sites, select each button and use the page picker to connect it to the matching page. Do not leave buttons pointing to a placeholder or an unrelated destination.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Button label</th><th>Place it on</th><th>Link it to</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody id="links"></tbody></table></div>
<div class="note"><b>Button-label rule:</b> use a label that describes the destination or action, such as “Browse Scholarships,” “Compare Locations,” or “Build My Action Plan.” Avoid vague labels such as “Click Here” unless the surrounding context makes the action unmistakable.</div>
</section>
<section class="panel">
<h2>Site-wide content standard</h2>
<details open><summary>Every page should answer five questions</summary><ol><li><b>Purpose:</b> What problem does this page help the visitor solve?</li><li><b>Useful information:</b> What facts, requirements, examples, and constraints matter?</li><li><b>Resources:</b> Where can visitors verify details or take action?</li><li><b>Decision:</b> What should a visitor compare, choose, or prepare?</li><li><b>Next step:</b> Which related page or action should the visitor visit next?</li></ol></details>
<details><summary>Facts, patterns, comparisons, and decision frameworks</summary><p>Place this material in the Intelligence Center, then link it from the relevant topic pages. Avoid duplicating long research explanations on every page. Each topic page should contain a short summary plus a link to the full analysis.</p></details>
<details><summary>Resources and links quality checks</summary><ul><li>Prefer official organization, school, government, and program pages for eligibility, deadlines, fees, and applications.</li><li>Label external links so visitors know what opens.</li><li>When a deadline or offer changes frequently, display a “last checked” date.</li><li>Do not describe an opportunity as currently open unless its current status has been checked.</li><li>Test every button on the published version, including mobile.</li></ul></details>
</section>
</main>
<footer class="wrap">PathKeeper Page Content & Link Map • Planning draft. This file does not connect to Google Sites, modify the live site, or publish edits.</footer>
